## 7. Stage G: key provisioning limitations

The frame key (K_frame) and heartbeat key (K_hb) are **prototype keys**. This section describes the arrangement as it is; no key was generated, replaced or printed.

| Question | Answer (verified 2026-11-07) |
|---|---|
| Where are the keys stored? | In plaintext as C initializers in `optee_os/core/arch/arm/plat-rpi3/rpi3_can_monitor_keys.h`, which only `rpi3_can_monitor.c` includes. They are compiled into read-only data of the Secure World image: **K_frame and K_hb each appear exactly once in the raw bytes** of `tee.elf`, `tee.bin` and `rpi3_can_monitor.o` of the Monitor-ON and debug builds (and twice in the host test binary); in the Monitor-OFF build they appear **zero** times. K_test (for Linux-side test tools) is defined in the header but is **not** instantiated in the logic file and does not appear in any binary (0 occurrences); its macro text appears only in debug-info macro data of the `.elf` and `.o` files |
| Who can read them? | Anyone who can read this working tree or its build outputs: the header, `FINAL_DIFF.patch` (contains the whole header), every scratch build under `build_scratch/`, and the host test binary. Files are mode `rw-rw-r--` (readable by every local user), the tree is not encrypted, and nothing is access controlled. Any future backup or copy of `Part_E_Verification/` also copies them. They are **not in git history**: the repository tracks none of these files, but a careless `git add -A` would add them |
| Why does repository or root access expose them? | A key that is a compile-time constant is part of the source and of every image built from it. Whoever holds the source, a build output, or a flashed image owns both HMAC keys and can forge any frame tag or heartbeat answer. On the board the image is in the FAT boot partition (`armstub8.bin`), which a root user on Linux can read from the card |
| Is Secure World memory a protection on this board? | **No.** The Raspberry Pi 3 has no TZASC, so a compromised Normal World can read Secure World RAM directly, regardless of where the keys came from. This is the already-recorded limit (no hardware isolation claim; detection only) and it applies to the keys too |
| Why is this not production-grade provisioning? | Same key in every build and every device; keys in the source tree; no per-device uniqueness; no rotation or revocation; no protected storage; no authenticated key injection; HMAC is symmetric so the CAN sender must also hold K_frame |
| What future provisioning work is required? | Not designed or done here. A minimum list for a later component: per-device random keys generated off-tree and injected at flash time (or derived from a hardware-unique source if the platform has one, which the Pi 3 does not have in a secure form); removal of keys from the repository and build outputs; a defined sender-side key distribution; rotation and revocation rules; and an honest statement of what an attacker with Normal-World root can read on this hardware |

## 8. Proposed fix for the H1 defect (SUPERSEDED: approved and applied, see section 13)

1. **Change:** in `rpi3_mon_frame_store` (`rpi3_can_monitor.c`), copy through a volatile destination pointer so the compiler cannot convert the loop into `memcpy`: add `volatile uint8_t *vd = dst;` and write `vd[i] = src[i];`. This is the same technique `rpi3_mon_build_proj0/1` already use (their loops did not become `memset`).
2. **Trial already done on a temporary copy outside the tree** (`source_tests/h1_fix_trial_closure_20261107.txt`): compiled with the ON build's own flags, `rpi3_mon_cmd17` then contains exactly **one** `memcpy` (the copy-in before the lock) instead of two. Source hash unchanged after the trial.
3. **Then:** rebuild ON, OFF and debug in scratch directories, rerun `hold_memcpy_check.py` (expect no call inside a hold), the 406 host tests, mutation testing and the static checks, and **add the object-code check to `static_checks.sh`** so the problem cannot return silently (`frame_equal`, the 44-byte compare, did not become `memcmp`, but nothing guards it either).
4. **Alternative if you prefer not to touch the loop:** `__attribute__((optimize("no-tree-loop-distribute-patterns")))` on the function. Slightly more fragile across compiler versions.
5. **Risk of the defect as it stands:** functionally harmless (44 bytes, bounded, no allocation); it adds one instrumented call to the cmd17 hold and breaks the rule written for exactly this reason. Nothing is claimed about its microsecond cost.

